`══►` = hard gate (cannot cross until upstream lands). `──►` = feeds/rework-forcing.

### 2a. The binding edges — physically-hard vs rework-forcing

Integrated across both tracks, the schedule is rigid at exactly these edges. **Physically-hard** = the
downstream literally cannot be built/run earlier. **Rework-forcing** = you *can* go out of order, but you
throw work away — same management burden in practice.

| # | Edge | Direction | Class | Why |
|---|------|-----------|-------|-----|
| **I-1** | 0.8.8 **EXP-OBS** (per-arm provenance + score breakdown) → odd **agent-signal / EXP-AF** (0.8.9 half, 0.8.11) | even → **odd** | **physically hard** | The agent-relevance signal judges *against* the provenance/score-breakdown + telemetry; nothing agent-feedback can run before it exists. |
| **I-2** | 0.8.14 **EXP-S** substrate → 0.8.15 **EXP-Fr** in-library dispatcher (no 0.8.13) | even → **odd** | **physically hard** | The dispatcher is in-library code over the kind-tagged indexes — no substrate, no in-library router. **This is the long pole.** |
| **I-3** | 0.8.7 **GPU device seam (#3)** → 0.8.16 **ONNX (#4)** | **odd → even** | rework-forcing | ONNX extends `resolve_device()` from #3; build #3 first or build the seam twice. *This edge breaks the "evens are self-contained" model.* |
| **I-4** | 0.8.7 (#3) + 0.8.16 (#4) → 0.8.18 **vector-equivalence (#5)** | mixed → even | physically hard-ish | The guard is meaningless with one backend — it needs ≥2 backends that can read each other's index. |
| **I-5** | 0.8.6 **#8 provider protocol** → 0.8.12 **#7/#6** | even → even | rework-forcing | Build consolidation before the unified protocol → throwaway contract + rewrite (the one edge that "bites mid-implementation"). |
| **I-6** | 0.8.14 EXP-S (slice 5) → F5 (slice 10) | within-release | rework-forcing | F5 rides EXP-S's field columns; one coordinated migration. |
| **I-7** | 0.8.16 Slice-15 (candle↔ONNX Δ) → 0.8.18 Slice-0 (#5 tolerance calibration) | even → even | **physically hard** | The vector-equivalence guard's tolerance cannot be calibrated before the cross-backend Δ it must tolerate has been measured. **Recorded retrospectively** (both releases are ✅ COMPLETE, so this edge is historical, not scheduling): it was documented in the plans but never promoted to this table. Ruled 2026-07-03 (`c6d3449a`, **M18** in `dev/design/fathomdb-memex-overall-roadmap/00-priorities-and-misalignments.md`); the paired half of that ruling — renaming the duplicate `F-11` to `F-11a` — landed in DOC-HYGIENE-2 T1c (`f9654a79`). **M18 is now fully discharged.** |

**Everything else floats.** GPU just accelerates re-embeds everywhere (soft); Gate-0/Gate-2 / EXP-A /
EXP-M4 / EXP-B′ / EXP-Fr-acc are $0 eval that can land anywhere in their window *as long as they're done
by their consumer's deadline* (§5); CI-micro (0.8.9) is only "recommended-before" GA. **(Superseded — see
F-11: the "everything-else-floats" model FAILED — unowned float was silently dropped and never ran; the
ladder is RETIRED as float and folded into 0.8.11 as owned, gated work.)**

### 2b. Can the even line be batched ahead of the odd line? — No

Taking both tracks unedited and integrated, **neither parity can be completed as a batch before the
other**, because the hard edges run **both directions**:

- **All-even-first is impossible** — edge **I-3**: even 0.8.16 (ONNX) needs odd 0.8.7 (GPU seam).
- **All-odd-first is impossible** — edges **I-1, I-2**: odd 0.8.15 (dispatcher) needs even 0.8.14
  (EXP-S); the odd agent-signal track needs even 0.8.8 (EXP-OBS).

The correct mental model is **one interleaved DAG**, not two parallel tracks. **The even/odd split is a
*labeling*** (in-band mechanism vs out-of-band experiment), **not a dependency boundary.** What must be
managed is the **landing order of ~6 real builds** (EXP-OBS, EXP-S, EXP-Fr, and the GPU→ONNX→vec-equiv
embedder chain, plus the provider protocol); all the planner-router *experiments* are $0 float scheduled
against those landings. **(Float-scheduling RETIRED — see F-11: the experiments never ran as float and are
now owned work folded into 0.8.11.)** The forced minimum interleave is:
`0.8.6 → 0.8.7 → 0.8.8 → 0.8.12 → 0.8.14 → 0.8.15 → 0.8.16 → 0.8.18`, with the $0 experiments slotted
into the 0.8.7 / 0.8.9 / 0.8.11 windows.

